new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a120c0d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,122 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy on a single server
+
+Fizzy can be self-hosted on one Linux server with Docker using
+[Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/). The configuration shipped in
+`config/deploy.yml` (with secrets in `.kamal/secrets`) runs everything
+in a single container:
+
+- the web app, with background jobs — including recurring jobs from
+  `config/recurring.yml` — running inside it via Solid Queue in Puma,
+  so there is no second job or database container to manage;
+- SQLite for the primary, cable, cache and queue databases, and local
+  disk for uploaded files, all under `/rails/storage`;
+- HTTPS at your own hostname, terminated by kamal-proxy with an
+  automatic Let's Encrypt certificate.
+
+No external database, Redis, object storage or container-registry
+account is required: Kamal pushes the image through a local registry
+that it tunnels to your server.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- A fork (or clone) of this repository that you can push your edits to.
+- A Linux server you can SSH into as `root` (or another user that can
+  run Docker/sudo), with ports 80 and 443 reachable from the internet.
+  Docker does not need to be installed — `kamal setup` installs it.
+- A DNS record for the hostname you want (e.g. `fizzy.example.com`)
+  pointing at that server. This must be in place before the first
+  deploy, or the Let's Encrypt challenge cannot succeed.
+- An SMTP server you can send mail through. Fizzy signs people in with
+  emailed magic links, so without working outbound mail nobody —
+  including you — can log in.
+
+## 1. Fill in `config/deploy.yml`
+
+Edit the placeholders in `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+| Placeholder | Replace with |
+| --- | --- |
+| `192.168.0.1` under `servers.web` | Your server's IP address or SSH hostname |
+| `fizzy.example.com` for `proxy.host` | Your public hostname |
+| `fizzy.example.com` for `APP_HOST` | The same public hostname (it builds the links in emails) |
+| `fizzy.example.com` for `SMTP_DOMAIN` | Your hostname, or the domain your mail provider tells you to use |
+| `smtp.example.com` for `SMTP_ADDRESS` | Your mail server's SMTP hostname |
+| `587` for `SMTP_PORT` | Your mail server's SMTP port, if different |
+| `fizzy@example.com` for `SMTP_USERNAME` and in `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` | The SMTP login, and the address mail should come from |
+
+Notes on the mail settings:
+
+- If your provider authenticates differently, adjust
+  `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` (`plain`, `login` or `cram_md5`) and set
+  `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO` to `"false"` if it does not use STARTTLS.
+- If your mail server needs no authentication (e.g. a local relay on
+  port 25), clear `SMTP_USERNAME`/`SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` and remove
+  `SMTP_PASSWORD` from the `env.secret` list.
+- Change `builder.arch` to `arm64` and `ssh.user` if your server is
+  ARM-based or you do not SSH in as `root`.
+
+## 2. Set your secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` contains no credentials itself: it reads them from
+your local environment. Export them in the shell you will deploy from
+(or adapt `.kamal/secrets` to fetch them from your password manager —
+don't commit real values):
+
+```sh
+# Signs cookies/sessions. Generate one and keep the same value for
+# every future deploy, or everyone gets signed out.
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(bin/rails secret)
+
+# The password for SMTP_USERNAME above.
+export SMTP_PASSWORD='your-smtp-password'
+
+# Enables browser push notifications. Generate a pair once with:
+#   bin/rails runner 'k = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{k.public_key}"; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{k.private_key}"'
+export VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY='the generated public key'
+export VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY='the generated private key'
+```
+
+If `bundle install` has not been run on your machine yet, use any long
+random string for `SECRET_KEY_BASE` instead, e.g.
+`openssl rand -hex 64`.
+
+## 3. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This bootstraps Docker on the server, starts kamal-proxy, builds and
+pushes the image, prepares the databases on first boot, and starts the
+app. When it finishes, Fizzy is at `https://<your-hostname>` — open it
+and sign up with your email address; the sign-in link arrives via the
+SMTP server you configured. Verify that email arrives before inviting
+anyone else, as it is the only way to log in.
+
+## Data and day-two operations
+
+All persistent data lives in the Docker volume named `fizzy`, mounted
+at `/rails/storage` in the container: the SQLite databases are there,
+and uploaded files are in its `production/files` directory. Kamal
+reuses this volume on every deploy, so data, accounts and files
+survive `bin/kamal deploy` (use that, after pulling new code, to
+upgrade). The volume is only removed if you run `kamal remove` or
+delete it with Docker — don't, unless you mean to start over.
+
+Back the volume up from the server before upgrades, for example:
+
+```sh
+docker run --rm -v fizzy:/storage -v "$PWD":/backup busybox \
+  tar czf /backup/fizzy-storage-backup.tar.gz -C /storage .
+```
+
+Useful commands from your checkout (all accept the same exported
+secrets as above):
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app logs -f     # follow app logs
+bin/kamal app details     # show the running container
+bin/kamal console         # Rails console on the server
+bin/kamal shell           # shell in a new container
+bin/kamal deploy          # deploy a new version
+```
diff --git a/test/deployment_config_test.rb b/test/deployment_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..50ec5e9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deployment_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "yaml"
+
+class DeploymentConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  setup do
+    @config = YAML.load_file(Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))
+  end
+
+  test "deploys a single web container to a single host" do
+    assert_equal "fizzy", @config["service"]
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], @config["servers"].keys
+    assert_equal 1, @config["servers"]["web"].size
+    assert_nil @config["accessories"]
+  end
+
+  test "serves the app over SSL from the proxy at the configured host" do
+    assert_equal true, @config.dig("proxy", "ssl")
+    assert @config.dig("proxy", "host").present?
+    assert_equal @config.dig("proxy", "host"), @config.dig("env", "clear", "APP_HOST")
+  end
+
+  test "persists SQLite data and uploads on a storage volume" do
+    assert_includes @config["volumes"], "fizzy:/rails/storage"
+    assert_equal "sqlite", @config.dig("env", "clear", "DATABASE_ADAPTER")
+  end
+
+  test "runs background jobs in the web container instead of a second container" do
+    assert_equal "true", @config.dig("env", "clear", "SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA")
+    assert_not @config["servers"].key?("jobs")
+  end
+
+  test "configures a mail server with its password kept secret" do
+    clear = @config.dig("env", "clear")
+    secret = @config.dig("env", "secret")
+
+    assert clear["SMTP_ADDRESS"].present?
+    assert clear["SMTP_PORT"].present?
+    assert clear["MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS"].present?
+    assert_includes secret, "SMTP_PASSWORD"
+    assert_includes secret, "SECRET_KEY_BASE"
+    assert_not clear.key?("SMTP_PASSWORD")
+  end
+
+  test "secrets file only references secrets from the environment" do
+    secrets = File.read(Rails.root.join(".kamal/secrets"))
+
+    @config.dig("env", "secret").each do |name|
+      assert_includes secrets, "#{name}=$#{name}"
+    end
+  end
+end
